## Weaknesses

W1. **The practical relevance of the main result is unclear due to the polynomial space requirement.** The headline result of query time ≈ 1/µ^{0.051} requires space ≈ 1/µ^{4.15}, which is an extremely large space overhead. For typical values of µ (say, µ = n^{-1}), this means space ≈ n^{4.15}, far exceeding the dataset size. The paper does not discuss at what parameter regimes this tradeoff would be practically meaningful, nor does it provide any empirical evaluation to validate that the theoretical improvements translate to wall-clock speedups. As noted in a review of a related kernel computation paper: *"How practical is the algorithm? It would be nice to actually see the algorithm in action with some wall clock times for real or synthetic data...Subquadratic time complexity is not the same as a fast algorithm"* (wLnls9LS3x, Reviewer 2). Similarly, another review on a theoretical data structure for kernel problems noted: *"no experimental results are reported. Adding experiments would improve the significance and impact of this work"* (DjtJV3ke1j, Reviewer 1).

W2. **Heavy reliance on numerical optimization with no closed-form analytical results.** The exponents 0.051, 0.1865, and 4.15 are all obtained numerically, and the paper acknowledges that *"The exact optimum does not seem simple to obtain analytically, and we therefore resort to numerics."* The tradeoff function ξ(δ) is defined via a nested min-max optimization (Equation 10) that is evaluated numerically rather than characterized analytically. This makes it difficult to develop intuition about the tradeoff curve, understand the sensitivity to parameter perturbations, or verify the numerical claims independently. A review of a related theoretical kernel paper raised similar concerns about the gap between theoretical bounds and practice: *"I would imagine that the theoretical bounds...are quite loose. Would it be possible to add a comparison of the theoretical bounds alongside the empirically realized errors?"* (QjSOgxJ0hp, Reviewer 3).

W3. **The core technique is primarily an adaptation/instantiation of existing frameworks rather than a fundamentally new construction.** The paper builds directly on the reduction framework of Charikar et al. (2020) (which reduces KDE to logarithmically many ANN problems), and substitutes the symmetric LSH of Andoni & Indyk (2008) with the asymmetric LSH of Andoni et al. (2017). The main technical work lies in optimizing the ρ_q, ρ_s parameters per distance scale—which is important but amounts to parameter optimization within an existing framework rather than a new algorithmic paradigm. A review of a closely related paper on KDE data structures raised similar concerns: *"Converting static algorithms into dynamic data structures does not seem to require significantly new ideas, but it is not trivial either"* (tra8ktyk0E, Reviewer 3). And another reviewer noted: *"The core techniques...are slight adaptations of the ones developed in [Charikar et al. FOCS20]"* (wLnls9LS3x, Reviewer 4).

W4. **The results are limited to the Gaussian kernel with no discussion of extensibility.** The paper focuses exclusively on the Gaussian kernel, and does not discuss whether the asymmetric LSH approach yields improvements for other commonly used kernels (e.g., Laplace, exponential, polynomial). This limits the generality of the contribution. A review of a related kernel transform paper raised a similar concern: *"The method is mainly tailored specifically for Gaussian kernels...It is still unclear if it allows other important kernels such as polynomial kernels"* (RtfXKkIJP1, Reviewer 3).

W5. **The linear-space result (δ=0) is only marginally better than data-dependent LSH of Charikar et al. (2020) while being data-independent, but this advantage is not thoroughly discussed.** The query time exponent of 0.1865 in the linear space regime is presented as slightly worse than the data-dependent 0.173 of Charikar et al. (2020), with the claimed advantage being simplicity. However, the paper does not provide a detailed comparison of the concrete overhead (hidden poly(d), poly(log), and poly(1/ε) factors) between the two approaches, which could substantially narrow or eliminate the gap in practice. Related reviews have emphasized the importance of concrete overhead: *"would it be possible to add a comparison of the theoretical bounds alongside the empirically realized errors"* (QjSOgxJ0hp, Reviewer 3).
</weaknesses>

